scenarios.modelAnswer:
Three holes, one of which the author explicitly talked himself out of.

**1. `status` is concatenated into a string literal.** `WHERE status = '${status}'` is the textbook case: `?status=paid' OR '1'='1` turns the predicate into a tautology, and a payload that appends `--` or `/*` discards the rest of the clause. Nothing here parses the value as data, so the fix is `WHERE status = $1` with `status` in the values array. Escaping quotes by hand is not the fix — it is a per-dialect, per-context guess that breaks on a different driver or a numeric context, and it is not what a reviewer can enumerate.

**2. `ids` is a list of *values* joined into SQL text.** The comment shows the misconception: ids arrive as strings from a query parameter, and `id IN (${idList.join(',')})` gives the database raw SQL regardless of what the caller typed. `?ids=1) OR (1=1` or `?ids=(SELECT id FROM orders)` is a working injection. Binding a list is not one placeholder: generate one marker per element (`$${n}`, `$${n+1}`, …) and push the values onto the same array. Coercing each id with `Number`/`Number.isInteger` is a useful complement — it rejects the payload early — but the parameter binding is the control.

**3. `direction` is an identifier slot with no constraint.** `sort` is validated against a list (correct), yet `direction` is interpolated raw. `?direction=desc, (SELECT ...)` or `desc NULLS LAST` changes the ORDER BY expression, and the same class of payload can be used for blind inference. It is a structural choice, so map it: `{ asc: 'ASC', desc: 'DESC' }` with a default. `LIMIT` is the mildest: `Number(limit) || 50` stops text injection but accepts `-1` and absurd values, so clamp with `Math.min(Math.max(Number.parseInt(limit, 10) || 50, 1), 200)` and bind it (PostgreSQL accepts `LIMIT $n`).

**Rewrite**

```ts
const COLUMNS = { id: 'id', total: 'total', created_at: 'created_at' } as const;
const DIRECTIONS = { asc: 'ASC', desc: 'DESC' } as const;

app.get('/api/orders', async (req, res) => {
  const { status = 'paid', ids = '', sort = 'created_at', direction = 'desc', limit = '50' } =
    req.query as Record<string, string>;

  const column = COLUMNS[sort as keyof typeof COLUMNS] ?? COLUMNS.created_at;
  const order = DIRECTIONS[direction as keyof typeof DIRECTIONS] ?? DIRECTIONS.desc;
  const pageSize = Math.min(Math.max(Number.parseInt(limit, 10) || 50, 1), 200);

  const params: unknown[] = [status];
  const idList = ids.split(',').map(Number).filter(Number.isInteger);
  let idFilter = '';
  if (idList.length > 0) {
    const markers = idList.map((_, i) => `$${params.length + i + 1}`);
    params.push(...idList);
    idFilter = `AND id IN (${markers.join(', ')})`;
  }
  params.push(pageSize);

  const sql = `
    SELECT id, customer_id, total, status, created_at
    FROM orders
    WHERE status = $1
      ${idFilter}
    ORDER BY ${column} ${order}
    LIMIT $${params.length}
  `;

  const { rows } = await pool.query(sql, params);
  res.json(rows);
});
```

**Why the shape is now reviewable.** Every `$n` is a value; the only interpolations left are `column`, `order` and the generated marker list, and all three are produced by application code from fixed maps or the array length — never from the request. Add the usual depth: the database role for this endpoint needs `SELECT` on `orders` only, so a future mistake cannot reach other tables; keep validation (enum status, integer ids, capped page size) as an early rejection layer, not as the defense; and add a regression test that sends `' OR '1'='1` and `ids=1) OR (1=1` and asserts a 400 or an empty result rather than a full table.

References: OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet (https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html) and PostgreSQL PREPARE (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-prepare.html).
